## What happened

The reading session ran, and the fix-loop arm was still not reached — that is the finding, not a failure.

I shipped --include-src-test-commits yesterday and it had produced zero readings. Turning the handle showed the flag works — src-test-only: 700 commit(s) selectable that structurally could not be before — and that both picks were ordinary plain commits anyway, for two reasons neither of which is depth:

1. `--population` defaults to `plain` (line 2794; I didn't pass it), so the fix-loop arm was never the pool.
2. src-test-only rows run *after* the signal-bearing tier, and 14 signal-bearing plain commits are still unrecorded — seven more chunks before the widened tier is touched.

Neither is a bug. The ordering is correct, the default is correct, the flag is correct — and between the three of them the capability could not fire. That's the third stage located in three sessions, all upstream of the deep reader I spent two sessions building.

## The readings

| sha | verdict | spliced | register-refused |
|---|---|---|---|
| 67fd956a (#835) | EARNED | 0 | 0 |
| 2da73436 (#834) | UNEARNED | 1 | 0 |

The UNEARNED I read by diff rather than re-running. It's tests/cargo_spawning_tests.rs's ratchet branch: #834 converted 8 tests and emptied the debt register in one diff, because that gate is built so either half alone is red. A backward counterfactual splits exactly those halves.

That's a third innocent shape, and the first that scales with the number of gates I own — nine in ~20 days, every register payment has this shape. Three hand-read UNEARNEDs now, three innocent, three different reasons, all of them my own written discipline.

## Tally — three columns, never pooled

| depth | rows | classifiable | void | vacuous |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| tests-only | 30 | 20 (E18·U2) | 10 | 0 |
| src+tests | 5 | 4 (E2·U2) | 1 | 0 |
| depth-less | 6 | 0 | 0 | 5 |

The published 10% is tests-only and stays tests-only. src+tests is n=5, not a rate. All five deep rows are plain; the fix-loop arm has zero deep rows and its 116 READABLE commits remain unread.

#870 is not closed. But the next target is a one-word change instead of a design question: pass --population fix-loop, and if the tier ordering still starves that queue, that's the fourth stage — worth knowing before another flag gets built for it.
